# `kk`

`kk` performs a Kramers-Kronig transform of a real tabulated function. It
interpolates the samples, subtracts the singular value, and evaluates the
remaining integral with GSL QAG.

## Usage

```text
kk [options] input output
kk [options] -
```

The second form reads two-column `x y` data from standard input and writes to
standard output. Input is sorted by `x`; it must contain an even number of
points on a grid symmetric around zero. The result is evaluated on that grid.

## Options

| Option | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `-h`, `--help` | Print help. |
| `-i METHOD`, `--interpolation METHOD` | Select `linear`, `cspline`, or `akima`. Default: `akima`. |
| `--epsabs VALUE` | Set the absolute QAG tolerance. Default: `1e-12`. |
| `--epsrel VALUE` | Set the relative QAG tolerance. Default: `1e-8`. |
| `--workspace-limit N` | Set the positive QAG workspace subdivision limit. Default: `1000`. |
| `--quadrature-rule RULE` | Select `15`, `21`, `31`, `41`, `51`, or `61`. Default: `15`. |
| `--gsl-error-policy POLICY` | Select `ignore`, `warn`, or `fail`. Default: `ignore`. |

`linear` is piecewise linear and requires at least two points. `cspline` is a
natural cubic spline and requires at least three points. `akima` is a local
piecewise-cubic interpolant and requires at least five points. Both cubic
methods can overshoot between samples and do not preserve positivity; use
`linear` when range or positivity preservation is required.

The QAG rule number is the number of Gauss-Kronrod points used on each adaptive
subinterval. Higher-order rules can help on smooth functions; lower-order rules
can leave more work to adaptive subdivision near local difficulties. The
workspace limit bounds the number of stored subintervals. Tolerances must be
finite and nonnegative and must form a combination accepted by GSL.

The error policy applies to a nonzero QAG status or a nonfinite result or error
estimate. `ignore` silently uses GSL's returned result, preserving legacy
behavior. `warn` writes a warning to standard error for each failed transform
point and continues. `fail` aborts with a nonzero exit status. Other input and
runtime errors are always fatal.

The displayed spline `Sum` is computed separately by
`gsl_spline_eval_integ`. QAG tolerances, rule, workspace, and error policy do
not control that operation.
